WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.560
All right, so today we're going to continue our discussion about how to establish a relationship

00:11.560 --> 00:20.000
between points in the real world and their projection onto the image plane.

00:20.000 --> 00:29.920
The reason why we want to establish this relation is that once we get the images of the environment,

00:29.920 --> 00:34.240
we want to use this relationship to go in the opposite direction, meaning once we have

00:34.240 --> 00:40.880
images, how do we reconstruct the real world leveraging these relationships.

00:40.880 --> 00:48.280
So the idea I discussed last time is to break down the projection of a point in the real

00:48.280 --> 00:54.560
world into the point on the image plane into three steps.

00:54.560 --> 01:02.000
First, we want to understand how to map a point P expressed with respect to the camera

01:02.000 --> 01:05.640
reference frame onto the image plane.

01:05.640 --> 01:13.680
Second, we want to understand how to then transform that projected point into coordinates

01:13.680 --> 01:18.120
with respect to the camera system in terms of pixel coordinates.

01:18.120 --> 01:22.680
And finally, we want to find the relation between the point P when expressed with respect

01:22.680 --> 01:28.600
to the camera reference frame and the same point when expressed with respect to a global

01:28.600 --> 01:29.600
reference frame.

01:29.600 --> 01:30.600
Yeah.

01:30.600 --> 01:31.600
I haven't started yet.

01:31.600 --> 01:32.600
Do you have any other questions?

01:32.600 --> 01:33.600
I have a more general question.

01:33.600 --> 01:39.600
Why are we spending so much time learning about the ways to invert these camera views

01:39.600 --> 01:44.600
when those modern cameras already invert the picture for us and just play it correctly

01:44.600 --> 01:46.600
as inverted pixels that we can interact with?

01:46.600 --> 01:47.600
Right.

01:47.600 --> 01:49.000
So you get the pixel, absolutely.

01:49.000 --> 01:53.880
And then once you have those images, you want to reconstruct the environment.

01:53.880 --> 01:58.680
And that procedure will require the relationship that we're going to cover, first of all.

01:58.680 --> 02:04.960
Second of all, in order for this procedure to be carried out, you need some, as we will

02:04.960 --> 02:10.600
see, some intrinsic parameters for the camera in terms of the focal length, pixel resolution,

02:10.600 --> 02:11.600
and so on.

02:11.600 --> 02:12.600
And you have specs.

02:12.600 --> 02:18.920
But those specs usually are not due to a number of reasons, manufacturing reasons and so on.

02:18.920 --> 02:24.800
And so on are not precise enough to carry out this process, which basically means that

02:24.800 --> 02:30.560
every roboticist, before starting using a camera for environmental construction, has

02:30.560 --> 02:35.280
to do a calibration process for a camera or for any sensor.

02:35.280 --> 02:40.000
And trust me, calibration is one of the most annoying and tedious processes we could do.

02:40.000 --> 02:44.940
And I can share with you offline a number of stories, for example, when I was calibrating

02:44.940 --> 02:49.740
cameras when I was working for NASA, brutal process.

02:49.740 --> 02:56.760
So I would like to give you some tips about how to do this so that if, in the future,

02:56.760 --> 03:01.260
you would have the task of reconstructing an environment or calibrating a camera, you

03:01.260 --> 03:06.260
don't get a shell shock as it did when it went to NASA, pretty much.

03:06.260 --> 03:07.260
Cool.

03:07.260 --> 03:08.260
All right.

03:08.260 --> 03:10.260
So I hope I motivated that.

03:10.260 --> 03:12.820
And this will become clearer later on today.

03:12.820 --> 03:20.380
So we already know how to do the first step, how to map a point in the 3D world, whose

03:20.380 --> 03:26.700
coordinates are expressed with respect to the camera reference frame, into a point on

03:26.700 --> 03:29.260
the image plane.

03:29.260 --> 03:37.140
And the mapping exploits the projection equation that we derived last time, whereby the x-coordinate

03:37.140 --> 03:44.660
in the image reference frame is equal to f, the focal length, the distance between the

03:44.660 --> 03:52.620
pinhole and the camera center, the x-coordinate with respect to the camera reference frame,

03:52.620 --> 03:55.240
and the z-coordinate with respect to the camera reference frame.

03:55.240 --> 03:57.740
So this is what we already discussed last time.

03:57.740 --> 04:01.460
Similar equation holds for the y-coordinate.

04:01.460 --> 04:06.020
We don't have a z-coordinate for small p, because the z-coordinate would always be equal

04:06.060 --> 04:08.100
to the focal length.

04:08.100 --> 04:12.980
The image plane is at fixed distance from the camera reference frame.

04:12.980 --> 04:20.780
Now that we have the coordinates for the point in the image plane, we want to relate those

04:20.780 --> 04:27.300
coordinates to the coordinates that a camera really uses, which basically are the pixel

04:27.300 --> 04:28.300
coordinates.

04:28.300 --> 04:32.140
In order to do so, we have to account for two facts.

04:32.620 --> 04:39.080
The coordinate system that the camera uses is not centered at the center of the image

04:39.080 --> 04:45.860
plane, but is actually centered at one of the corners, at the idea that basically you

04:45.860 --> 04:50.360
read pixel in a sort of row and column format.

04:50.360 --> 04:57.220
So just to make things clear, by looking at the image plane from this side, let's assume

04:57.220 --> 05:01.580
that this is the reference system that we've used for the image plane.

05:01.580 --> 05:06.700
The actual reference system that the camera would use is situated at a corner, say bottom

05:06.700 --> 05:07.700
right.

05:07.700 --> 05:12.420
It could also be up left and so on, but usually it's at the corner, which means that the origin

05:12.420 --> 05:17.460
is here, and then the coordinate changes as you go farther and farther from the coordinates.

05:17.460 --> 05:20.960
I mean, it's just a convention, but this is what it is.

05:20.960 --> 05:26.340
So how do we go from a reference system that is centered at the center of the image plane

05:26.340 --> 05:29.420
to a camera system that is centered at one of the corners?

05:29.700 --> 05:36.460
Well, let X0, Y0 be the coordinate of the origin of the camera reference system, or

05:36.460 --> 05:41.620
the image plane reference system, with respect to the camera reference system.

05:41.620 --> 05:50.420
Then the mapping between a point expressed with respect to the image reference frame

05:50.420 --> 05:57.580
and a point expressed in terms of the camera reference frame is simply related by a translation,

05:57.580 --> 06:02.740
and the translation is given by the coordinates of the center of the reference frame in the

06:02.740 --> 06:03.740
image plane.

06:03.740 --> 06:04.740
Yes?

06:04.740 --> 06:08.460
A little bit of a basic question, but why is the corner plane of the camera centered

06:08.460 --> 06:18.020
at the bottom versus the top, because iron pixels are usually centered at the top right

06:18.020 --> 06:19.020
corner?

06:19.020 --> 06:20.020
Oh, yeah.

06:20.020 --> 06:21.020
The important thing is that usually it is at the corner.

06:21.020 --> 06:22.020
Oh, OK.

06:22.020 --> 06:25.820
Here, just as an example, I'm not placing it here, but you're right, oftentimes it's

06:25.820 --> 06:26.820
at the top.

06:26.860 --> 06:31.540
The important thing is that you need to apply a translation in order to relate coordinates

06:31.540 --> 06:35.940
with respect to the reference system we've been using so far in terms of the reference

06:35.940 --> 06:38.460
system that the camera really uses.

06:38.460 --> 06:46.980
For example, if you have a point that, according to our previous reference system, is here,

06:46.980 --> 06:51.820
then the coordinates in the camera reference system will be X0 and Y0, because this will

06:51.820 --> 06:53.820
be 0 and this will be 0.

06:53.820 --> 06:55.980
Does that make sense?

06:55.980 --> 06:56.980
Any questions here?

06:56.980 --> 07:01.620
So we'll just apply the translation in terms of the reference system.

07:01.620 --> 07:02.620
Yeah?

07:02.620 --> 07:07.420
What would be the names of the coordinate frames so you can have it more clearly so

07:07.420 --> 07:08.420
the new...

07:08.420 --> 07:17.660
I will call this image plane reference frame, and this is the two-camera reference frame.

07:17.660 --> 07:22.060
I mean, it gets a little bit confusing, because this is then the camera reference frame, and

07:22.060 --> 07:23.060
then we have a warframe.

07:23.580 --> 07:32.020
In reality, the two that really matter are the warframe and the camera frame, because

07:32.020 --> 07:40.580
of course you read pixels in terms of the camera frame, and you reconstruct points in

07:40.580 --> 07:46.580
the real world with respect to an external reference frame.

07:46.580 --> 07:51.700
So these are just intermediate reference frames that simplify the derivation of these relations.

07:53.780 --> 07:54.780
Does that make sense?

07:58.100 --> 08:06.900
Okay, so the last step is that so far we have been considering continuous values for the

08:06.900 --> 08:07.900
coordinates.

08:07.900 --> 08:14.180
But in reality, a camera reasons in terms of pixel coordinates, so discrete coordinates

08:14.180 --> 08:18.020
for the points in the image.

08:18.060 --> 08:25.660
Which means that basically we have to discretize the values that we have completed so far in

08:25.660 --> 08:28.660
order to translate them into pixel coordinates.

08:28.660 --> 08:35.780
In particular, the idea is to consider two numbers, kx and ky, that represent the number

08:35.780 --> 08:41.100
of pixels per unit of distance in an image.

08:41.100 --> 08:46.580
And so a pixel that has... say that we have three pixels per centimeter.

08:46.980 --> 08:52.180
I mean, of course you have many more, but say that we have three pixels per centimeter.

08:52.180 --> 08:57.700
And say that x tilde is equal to one, one centimeter.

08:57.700 --> 09:03.740
Then the pixel coordinate is equal to three.

09:03.740 --> 09:08.820
So we basically multiply by the number of pixels per unit of distance, and then we apply

09:08.820 --> 09:09.820
rounding.

09:09.820 --> 09:10.820
Make sense?

09:10.820 --> 09:20.100
So for example, if the x coordinate is two centimeters, then the pixel coordinate will

09:20.100 --> 09:22.820
be six.

09:22.820 --> 09:31.300
And of course, there is in a way a loss of information because if we have three pixels

09:31.300 --> 09:37.540
per centimeter, then all those pixels that are within 0.33 centimeters are mapped to

09:37.540 --> 09:45.740
the same pixel value, which is standard discretization error.

09:45.740 --> 09:52.660
So with this understanding, then a point in the 3D world expressed with respect to the

09:52.660 --> 10:00.540
camera reference system, this reference system here, is then mapped into pixel coordinates

10:00.540 --> 10:01.540
u and v.

10:01.540 --> 10:06.980
u will be the horizontal pixel coordinate, v will be the vertical pixel coordinate as

10:06.980 --> 10:16.020
kx times the projection plus kx x tilde 0, where x tilde 0 and y 0 again are the coordinates

10:16.020 --> 10:20.100
of the reference system in the image plane with respect to the reference system that

10:20.100 --> 10:22.100
the camera really uses.

10:22.100 --> 10:23.100
Yeah?

10:23.100 --> 10:26.660
Is it common that kx and ky are the same when they often are not?

10:26.660 --> 10:28.220
Oftentimes they are different values.

10:28.220 --> 10:33.540
And oftentimes you have to estimate that from data through a process referred to as

10:33.540 --> 10:37.580
calibration that we're going to discuss later.

10:37.580 --> 10:43.580
So that's why here we refer to kx and ky differently as opposed to just say k.

10:43.580 --> 10:46.460
All right.

10:46.460 --> 10:55.780
So let's just call this product alpha, this product u0, this product v0, and this product

10:55.780 --> 10:57.300
beta.

10:57.300 --> 11:03.900
So we can clearly express this transformation according to the equations here.

11:03.900 --> 11:09.060
In general, alpha, beta, u0, and v0 are not quantities that you know or that you know

11:09.060 --> 11:13.500
in any way that is accurate enough, which basically will lead us to the explanation

11:13.500 --> 11:18.540
of the calibration procedure that allows you to infer those quantities from data.

11:18.540 --> 11:20.320
OK.

11:20.320 --> 11:27.460
So one thing here to note is that a projective transformation, as already emphasized the

11:27.460 --> 11:37.800
last time, is a non-linear transformation, non-linear because the z-coordinate is divided.

11:37.800 --> 11:43.880
So we cannot really express this transformation as the product of a matrix times a vector,

11:43.880 --> 11:48.220
which from a mathematical standpoint, it is an inconvenience.

11:48.220 --> 11:57.660
So one known trick in computer vision is to then consider a new coordinate system referred

11:57.660 --> 12:04.740
to as a homogeneous coordinate system, whereby one is able to transform the non-linear projection

12:04.740 --> 12:07.820
transformation into linear ones.

12:07.820 --> 12:12.240
The non-linearity, of course, does not magically disappear.

12:12.240 --> 12:16.560
It disappears in that the non-linearity is then taken into account when you transform

12:16.560 --> 12:21.120
back from homogeneous coordinates to non-homogeneous coordinates.

12:21.120 --> 12:25.320
In particular, what is a homogeneous coordinate system?

12:25.320 --> 12:34.680
So let's assume that you're given a point x, y in R2.

12:34.680 --> 12:48.440
And we associate each point x, y in R2 to a point in R3 written as x, y, 1 times lambda.

12:48.440 --> 12:52.000
So it's not a one-to-one mapping, it's a one-to-main mapping.

12:52.000 --> 12:59.560
So to each point in R2, we associate the points in R3 that are equal to x, y, and 1.

12:59.560 --> 13:02.280
So we add one dimension.

13:02.280 --> 13:11.480
And they are parameterized by a scalar lambda.

13:11.480 --> 13:12.820
So this seems very awkward.

13:12.820 --> 13:16.040
And the reason why we do this will become clear in a second.

13:16.040 --> 13:21.960
So for now, we're just establishing a correspondence between points in R2 to a class of points

13:21.960 --> 13:22.960
in R3.

13:22.960 --> 13:31.280
A class because we have a family of points in R3 parameterized by these coefficient lambda.

13:31.280 --> 13:38.400
So now, once you have, so this will be your original point in non-homogeneous coordinates.

13:38.400 --> 13:50.880
This is the point represented in the homogeneous coordinates.

13:50.880 --> 13:54.320
So we know how to go from non-homogeneous to homogeneous coordinates.

13:54.320 --> 14:02.160
How do we go back from homogeneous coordinates to non-homogeneous one?

14:02.160 --> 14:06.740
So as you can see from here, if you're given the vector in non-homogeneous coordinates,

14:06.740 --> 14:08.640
how do you reconstruct the vector?

14:08.640 --> 14:12.440
So if you're given a vector in homogeneous coordinates, how do you reconstruct the vector

14:12.440 --> 14:14.840
in homogeneous coordinates?

14:14.840 --> 14:17.440
By the value in the.

14:17.440 --> 14:18.440
Exactly.

14:18.440 --> 14:24.200
So basically, this will be lambda x, lambda y, lambda.

14:24.200 --> 14:32.560
So if I give you this point, in order to find x, what you do is you divide the first component

14:32.560 --> 14:42.320
by the third one and the second component by the third one, lambda y over lambda.

14:42.320 --> 14:53.480
Which basically means that if you're given in general a point x, y, w in R3, then it's

14:53.480 --> 14:59.920
non-homogeneous representation will be x over w and y over w.

14:59.920 --> 15:05.160
So that we have a correspondence between points in a non-homogeneous representation and points

15:05.160 --> 15:08.600
in a homogeneous representation.

15:08.600 --> 15:15.080
The important thing to note is that here we have an equivalency class of points that represent

15:15.080 --> 15:22.440
a single point in R2.

15:22.440 --> 15:27.160
The same thing can be done in R3 for what matters can be done for any vector in Rn,

15:27.160 --> 15:33.120
can be transformed into homogeneous coordinates in Rn plus 1.

15:33.120 --> 15:35.000
So this is just a mathematical construction.

15:35.000 --> 15:43.280
Right now, there are many good reasons why one would consider such new coordinates.

15:43.280 --> 15:50.160
The reason why we consider them is by considering the following.

15:50.200 --> 15:58.760
Let's consider the homogeneous representation of the point in the 3D world as represented

15:58.760 --> 16:03.120
with respect to the camera reference frame.

16:03.120 --> 16:07.320
So Pc.

16:07.320 --> 16:17.360
So we have Pc, which is given by xc, yc, zc.

16:17.400 --> 16:27.600
So it's a homogeneous representation that I refer to as a superscript h.

16:27.600 --> 16:42.160
Would be xc, yc, zc, sorry, 1 times lambda.

16:42.240 --> 16:57.760
So now, let's introduce the matrix alpha 0, u0 is the matrix that we have over there,

16:57.760 --> 16:59.200
and we have a 0.

16:59.200 --> 17:08.720
Then we have a 0, beta, v0, and then we have 0, and then we have 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0.

17:13.160 --> 17:18.960
So this matrix, as we will see later on, is referred to as the camera matrix K.

17:18.960 --> 17:30.000
And let's multiply this by the homogeneous representation of the 3D point times lambda.

17:30.000 --> 17:55.920
So this, when we carry out the product, what we get is alpha xc plus 0 plus u0 xc.

17:55.920 --> 18:04.640
Then we have beta yc plus v0 xc.

18:04.640 --> 18:17.520
And then we have zc times lambda, which is what we have there.

18:17.520 --> 18:22.120
We don't have the lambda, and I'll tell you in a second why that is the case.

18:22.840 --> 18:35.840
So then let's divide this vector by the third component.

18:35.840 --> 18:39.960
So what we get is, and we eliminate the third component.

18:39.960 --> 18:47.360
So what we get is the vector alpha xc over zc plus u0.

18:47.360 --> 18:54.080
And beta yc over zc plus v0.

18:58.080 --> 19:02.520
So you should recognize that this is really the projected point

19:02.520 --> 19:08.520
P in the pixel reference frame, which

19:08.560 --> 19:19.440
means that this was the point P in the pixel reference frame expressed

19:19.440 --> 19:22.160
in homogeneous coordinates.

19:22.160 --> 19:24.520
So this is the point of all of this, is

19:24.520 --> 19:32.040
that when we express both the 3D point in non-homogeneous coordinates

19:32.040 --> 19:36.280
and its projection in non, sorry, when we express both the 3D point

19:36.280 --> 19:39.080
in homogeneous coordinates and its projection

19:39.080 --> 19:42.760
in homogeneous coordinates, the projection transformation

19:42.760 --> 19:46.840
is just a linear transformation in matrix times a vector, which

19:46.840 --> 19:49.480
as we will see, simplifies a number of operations,

19:49.480 --> 19:53.800
including the process of camera calibration.

19:53.800 --> 19:56.600
Where did that non-linearity go?

19:56.600 --> 20:00.640
Well, the non-linearity went into the process

20:00.640 --> 20:04.480
of transforming back homogeneous coordinates into non-homogeneous

20:04.480 --> 20:05.600
ones.

20:05.640 --> 20:07.280
So the idea is to lift everything

20:07.280 --> 20:12.720
to homogeneous coordinates, do all our computation over there,

20:12.720 --> 20:14.280
now that everything is linear.

20:14.280 --> 20:16.680
And once we're done with that, we will go back

20:16.680 --> 20:19.880
to the true coordinates and non-homogeneous ones,

20:19.880 --> 20:21.960
and at that time, account for the non-linearity.

20:21.960 --> 20:25.840
So it's a clever way of exploiting linear transformations

20:25.840 --> 20:28.120
in a non-linear setting.

20:28.120 --> 20:30.840
Does that make sense?

20:30.840 --> 20:33.440
So the reason that here I didn't put lambda

20:33.440 --> 20:38.440
is that since homogeneous coordinates are an equivalency

20:38.440 --> 20:41.560
class, we can just pick one example,

20:41.560 --> 20:45.040
just fix lambda equal 1 instead of training this lambda.

20:45.040 --> 20:47.880
As you see, if I picked lambda 1 here,

20:47.880 --> 20:51.080
I would have obtained the same result, just

20:51.080 --> 20:53.080
for mathematical convenience.

20:53.080 --> 20:58.760
Now, this matrix here, where is it?

20:58.760 --> 20:59.260
Oops.

20:59.260 --> 21:14.380
This matrix here, the 3 by 3 matrix

21:14.380 --> 21:17.460
is referred to as the camera matrix.

21:17.460 --> 21:22.300
And as you can see, it contains some intrinsic parameters

21:22.300 --> 21:25.940
of a camera, so parameters that characterize

21:25.940 --> 21:31.420
the manufacturing characteristics of a camera.

21:31.420 --> 21:35.860
In particular, alpha and beta, for example,

21:35.860 --> 21:40.060
are related to the focal length and the pixel scaling.

21:40.060 --> 21:43.220
And u0 v0 is related to the position

21:43.220 --> 21:47.260
of the central reference system in the image plane.

21:47.260 --> 21:50.180
These are parameters that, in general,

21:50.180 --> 21:53.180
need to be estimated from data through a calibration procedure

21:53.180 --> 21:54.580
that we're going to discuss next.

21:56.940 --> 22:00.900
So then we can write the projection transformation

22:00.900 --> 22:04.620
by leveraging homogeneous coordinates in a linear fashion,

22:04.620 --> 22:08.500
whereby we have a matrix that accounts for the camera

22:08.500 --> 22:15.140
matrix and a column of zeros times

22:15.140 --> 22:21.620
the homogeneous representation of the point in the 3D world.

22:21.620 --> 22:23.140
Yeah?

22:23.140 --> 22:25.860
Maybe off the topic, but what does homogeneous

22:25.860 --> 22:28.940
mean in this context?

22:28.940 --> 22:35.580
So homogeneous coordinates have a much richer theory,

22:35.580 --> 22:38.420
I would say, a much broader application in terms

22:38.420 --> 22:40.260
of a projective geometry.

22:40.260 --> 22:42.220
For the sake of this class, we just

22:42.220 --> 22:44.860
use it as a convenient mathematical trick

22:44.860 --> 22:48.220
in order to express the projection transformation that

22:48.220 --> 22:51.860
is non-linear into a projection transformation that

22:51.900 --> 22:54.340
is linear in a lifted space, where

22:54.340 --> 22:57.060
we have augmented the coordinates

22:57.060 --> 22:59.220
with one additional dimension.

22:59.220 --> 23:01.660
So beautiful for the sake of this class

23:01.660 --> 23:03.460
as a mathematical trick.

23:03.460 --> 23:09.940
In reality, there are many more applications for this concept.

23:09.940 --> 23:11.900
Cool.

23:11.900 --> 23:15.380
In reality, these, and I'm not going

23:15.380 --> 23:20.900
to go too much into the details, but these number

23:20.940 --> 23:25.380
here sometimes is not zero and is referred to gamma

23:25.380 --> 23:27.620
and it presents the so-called skew parameter.

23:27.620 --> 23:29.740
Basically, it accounts for the fact

23:29.740 --> 23:35.940
that sometimes the axis of the reference frame

23:35.940 --> 23:40.860
that the camera uses are not exactly perpendicular.

23:40.860 --> 23:45.620
Usually, this is a quite small number for good cameras.

23:45.620 --> 23:46.980
So for the sake of this class, we

23:46.980 --> 23:49.460
will treat that gamma as a zero.

23:49.460 --> 23:52.940
But just for your knowledge, in general,

23:52.940 --> 23:55.460
one should also consider the skew parameter

23:55.460 --> 23:56.580
in the calibration process.

24:01.140 --> 24:01.740
All right.

24:01.740 --> 24:05.260
So now we can move to the topic of today,

24:05.260 --> 24:10.900
that is to complete this projection procedure by also

24:10.900 --> 24:14.900
counting for the transformation from the coordinates

24:14.900 --> 24:17.460
expressed in a word frame with respect

24:17.460 --> 24:20.820
to the coordinates in 3D as expressed

24:20.820 --> 24:22.460
in the camera reference frame.

24:28.780 --> 24:29.940
Any questions in the meantime?

24:36.940 --> 24:38.580
I'm going a little bit slower than usual

24:38.580 --> 24:43.100
because sometimes this concept of homogeneous coordinates

24:43.100 --> 24:45.620
is a little bit confusing at first.

24:52.580 --> 24:54.740
Yep.

24:54.740 --> 24:57.460
The camera matrix assumes a single focal length,

24:57.460 --> 24:58.980
but if you're refocusing the camera,

24:58.980 --> 25:00.420
that's what length changes, right?

25:00.420 --> 25:03.340
So you need to recompute the entire matrix every time

25:03.340 --> 25:04.180
you're doing that?

25:04.180 --> 25:06.820
In principle, it's if you change focal length in a way

25:06.820 --> 25:09.580
that cannot be characterized in other ways, yeah.

25:09.580 --> 25:12.420
All right.

25:12.420 --> 25:17.700
So today we're going to continue the procedure

25:17.700 --> 25:20.140
of establishing a relationship between a point in 3D

25:20.140 --> 25:22.060
and its projection in an image plane.

25:22.060 --> 25:23.420
But most importantly, we're going

25:23.420 --> 25:26.900
to talk about how to estimate from data

25:26.900 --> 25:30.460
the numbers that appear in the camera matrix.

25:30.460 --> 25:32.580
And we'll see whether this is useful.

25:32.580 --> 25:38.700
So as before, the setup is that we have a point in 3D,

25:38.700 --> 25:41.980
and we want to understand how it is mapped into pixel

25:41.980 --> 25:44.060
coordinates.

25:44.060 --> 25:46.580
Once again, to make sure that you don't get confused,

25:46.580 --> 25:48.060
so I refer to this reference system

25:48.060 --> 25:51.340
as the word reference system, this reference system

25:51.340 --> 25:56.060
as the camera reference frame, this reference system

25:56.060 --> 25:58.580
as the reference system in the image plane,

25:58.580 --> 26:00.580
and the reference system in terms of pixel

26:00.580 --> 26:03.020
as the pixel reference frame.

26:03.020 --> 26:04.700
At the end of the day, what we're interested

26:04.700 --> 26:06.660
is to go from the word reference frame

26:06.660 --> 26:08.300
to the pixel reference frame.

26:08.460 --> 26:11.180
We introduce two more reference frames

26:11.180 --> 26:15.340
just to ease the derivation.

26:15.340 --> 26:17.940
So the last step that we need to account for

26:17.940 --> 26:20.620
is how do we transform coordinates

26:20.620 --> 26:24.900
from the word reference frame to the camera reference frame.

26:24.900 --> 26:29.220
Once we do that, we put all this transformation together,

26:29.220 --> 26:31.820
and we have the end-to-end transformation from PW

26:31.820 --> 26:32.820
to Excels.

26:35.420 --> 26:36.020
All right.

26:36.020 --> 26:39.780
So to some of you, this might be well-known,

26:39.780 --> 26:42.140
but to some, others may not be well-known.

26:42.140 --> 26:47.620
So we're going to derive the relation that

26:47.620 --> 26:51.060
relates the coordinates of a point

26:51.060 --> 26:52.660
into different reference systems.

26:59.620 --> 27:04.140
So let's assume first for simplicity that,

27:04.140 --> 27:08.380
so here in this plot, this is the camera reference system,

27:08.380 --> 27:10.460
and this is the word reference system.

27:10.460 --> 27:12.660
So let's assume first for simplicity

27:12.660 --> 27:16.580
that the origin of the camera reference system

27:16.580 --> 27:20.100
and of the word reference system coincide.

27:20.100 --> 27:24.100
So in other words, this vector t is equal to 0.

27:24.100 --> 27:26.620
So we're in the situation whereby

27:26.620 --> 27:33.820
this will be the camera reference system.

27:33.820 --> 27:37.500
And then let's see if I can mimic the drawing there.

27:42.180 --> 27:48.900
This will be IW, this will be JW, and this will be KW.

27:48.900 --> 27:51.500
Just to be clear, I put underscore

27:51.500 --> 27:54.700
to denote that these are vectors.

27:54.700 --> 27:57.820
So these are the unit vectors along each of the axes.

27:57.820 --> 28:00.860
I is the unit vector along the x-axis,

28:00.860 --> 28:04.860
J is the unit vector along the y-axis, and so on.

28:04.860 --> 28:08.260
So we're given a point Q. So this is the origin.

28:08.260 --> 28:15.380
Say Q. And we express this point Q with respect

28:15.380 --> 28:19.660
to two different coordinate systems, the word reference

28:19.660 --> 28:22.020
system and the camera reference system.

28:22.020 --> 28:24.380
We want to understand how these coordinates are related.

28:24.380 --> 28:29.900
So if I give you the point expressed

28:29.900 --> 28:31.820
in terms of the word reference frame,

28:31.820 --> 28:34.580
how do I map those coordinates into the coordinates

28:34.580 --> 28:37.100
as expressed in terms of the camera reference frame?

28:41.380 --> 28:43.740
That's what we want to do.

28:43.740 --> 28:47.900
All right, so let's translate mathematically

28:47.900 --> 28:48.780
what we want to do.

28:48.780 --> 28:54.860
So Q, which is also a vector, so let me put it here.

28:54.860 --> 28:56.460
So just for simplicity, we're first

28:56.460 --> 28:59.060
considering the case where the origin of the word reference

28:59.100 --> 29:01.860
system coincides with the origin of the camera reference system.

29:01.860 --> 29:04.660
We'll then consider the case where they are separated.

29:04.660 --> 29:18.860
So we can write Q as QCXI plus QCYJ plus QCZK.

29:22.740 --> 29:25.340
C means that this is the coordinate with respect

29:25.340 --> 29:27.540
to the camera reference frame.

29:27.540 --> 29:29.700
X means that this is the coordinate with respect

29:29.700 --> 29:36.660
to the x-axis, where I represent the unit vector along the x-axis

29:36.660 --> 29:39.740
for the camera reference frame.

29:39.740 --> 29:42.340
Then I can also represent the Q with respect

29:42.340 --> 29:44.660
to a different coordinate system, so the word reference

29:44.660 --> 29:45.580
system.

29:45.580 --> 29:57.100
So in that case, I will have a QCWIW plus Q.

29:57.140 --> 30:11.780
QWXIW plus QWYJW plus a QWZKW, OK?

30:11.780 --> 30:15.860
So so far, I've done nothing but writing this point

30:15.860 --> 30:18.220
into different reference systems.

30:18.220 --> 30:22.620
Now, Q is the same point, which means

30:22.620 --> 30:28.100
that this vector has to be equal to this vector.

30:28.100 --> 30:39.620
So this means that this is also equal to QCXI plus QCYJ

30:39.620 --> 30:40.620
plus QCZK.

30:40.620 --> 30:54.620
So if I give you the coordinates of the point Q with respect

30:54.620 --> 30:58.540
to the word reference frame, and I tell you in the sense

30:58.540 --> 31:01.100
that I will make more explicit in a second what

31:01.100 --> 31:06.780
is the relation between I and IW, J and JW, and K and KW.

31:06.780 --> 31:09.660
So basically, how the camera's reference frame

31:09.660 --> 31:12.660
is rotated with respect to the word reference frame,

31:12.660 --> 31:15.300
how will we find the relation between the coordinates

31:15.300 --> 31:17.220
and camera reference frame and the coordinates

31:17.220 --> 31:18.420
and the word reference frame?

31:25.500 --> 31:29.900
So the idea or the trick is to multiply.

31:29.900 --> 31:33.460
So let's assume that we want to find

31:33.460 --> 31:36.780
a relation between the QCX and the coordinates

31:36.780 --> 31:38.500
in the word reference frame.

31:38.540 --> 31:43.260
So what we do is we multiply.

31:43.260 --> 31:44.780
So this is an inequality.

31:44.780 --> 31:46.940
So this is equal to this.

31:46.940 --> 31:52.860
We multiply both sides by I. So if I multiply this vector

31:52.860 --> 31:57.500
by I, multiply them into the dot product, what is the result?

32:01.020 --> 32:02.780
Yes, why?

32:02.780 --> 32:06.420
Because all the other components go away because they're orthonormal.

32:06.420 --> 32:11.220
So Y times K is 0 because Y is orthogonal to K.

32:11.220 --> 32:14.900
We selected the reference system in orthogonal basis.

32:14.900 --> 32:18.060
Y times J is 0, and Y times I is equal to 1

32:18.060 --> 32:22.500
because I is a unit vector in the x-axis direction.

32:22.500 --> 32:39.140
So we have QCX equal to QWXIW times I plus a QWY JW times

32:39.140 --> 32:51.420
I plus QWZ KW times I.

32:51.500 --> 32:54.300
And then I can do the same for the other coordinates.

32:54.300 --> 33:06.300
I will multiply this time by J. So QCY is equal to QWXIW times

33:06.300 --> 33:23.020
J plus QWY JY times J plus QWZ KWY times J.

33:23.020 --> 33:33.460
And then the same will be for QCZ, this time multiplied by K.

33:33.460 --> 33:39.300
So I can then represent these equations more compactly

33:39.300 --> 33:52.780
by writing a QCX, QCY, QCZ as some matrix that I denote as R

33:52.780 --> 34:03.260
times QCW, yeah, QWX, QWY, QWZ.

34:03.260 --> 34:05.240
So what is this matrix R?

34:05.240 --> 34:09.660
Well, we just read the rows from here.

34:09.660 --> 34:12.540
And that's exactly what you have over there.

34:17.340 --> 34:21.540
This is just pattern matching this with that.

34:21.540 --> 34:24.940
So for example, the first component will be,

34:24.940 --> 34:28.340
so first element of the first row is IW times I.

34:28.340 --> 34:32.260
Second element of the first row is JW times I, and so on.

34:36.100 --> 34:40.260
So this is to say that if we know all of these dot products,

34:40.260 --> 34:44.460
if you give me any point, if you give me

34:44.460 --> 34:47.820
the coordinates of any point in the word reference frame,

34:47.820 --> 34:49.660
by doing this multiplication, I can immediately

34:49.660 --> 34:53.540
find the coordinates in the camera reference frame.

34:53.540 --> 34:56.140
In particular, knowing these dot products

34:56.140 --> 34:59.740
implies that we know what are the relative angles

34:59.740 --> 35:04.940
between the basis vectors for the two coordinate reference

35:04.940 --> 35:06.940
systems, which basically means that we

35:06.940 --> 35:09.700
know what is the orientation of one reference system

35:09.700 --> 35:11.060
with respect to the other one.

35:11.060 --> 35:13.720
For this reason, this matrix R is

35:13.720 --> 35:17.060
referred to as a rotation matrix, which

35:17.060 --> 35:19.220
is the property of being an orthogonal matrix.

35:19.220 --> 35:21.860
By the way we constructed it, the vector

35:22.700 --> 35:24.380
the dot product between the vector

35:24.380 --> 35:28.020
are in between the columns are 0,

35:28.020 --> 35:32.820
and then there is the fact that the determinant is equal to 1.

35:32.820 --> 35:33.340
Cool.

35:33.340 --> 35:33.840
All right.

35:33.840 --> 35:35.580
So is this clear?

35:38.860 --> 35:42.340
You may already know this, but it doesn't hard to derive it.

35:42.340 --> 35:47.900
No, so what happens if now the two origins do not overlap?

35:47.900 --> 35:55.300
Now we can represent the point Pw, the point P in general,

35:55.300 --> 35:57.380
with respect to the camera reference frame,

35:57.380 --> 36:04.660
as the summation of P in the coordinate in the reference

36:04.660 --> 36:10.020
frame, in the camera reference frame, is equal to t plus q.

36:10.020 --> 36:17.460
So t, I just put an underscore there

36:17.460 --> 36:20.300
to emphasize that this is a vector.

36:20.300 --> 36:24.060
So t is the vector that points from the origin

36:24.060 --> 36:26.460
of the camera reference frame to the origin of the world

36:26.460 --> 36:29.460
reference frame.

36:29.460 --> 36:34.140
And q is the position of the point P with respect

36:34.140 --> 36:37.180
to the world reference frame.

36:37.180 --> 36:42.140
Now, assume that we know t.

36:45.220 --> 36:46.820
How can we express it?

36:46.820 --> 36:54.020
So q is the vector that expresses the point P with respect

36:54.020 --> 36:55.620
to the world reference frame.

36:55.620 --> 36:59.260
But we can think of translating this vector q to the camera

36:59.260 --> 37:02.140
reference frame, and then we recover the previous problem

37:02.140 --> 37:04.900
of mapping the vector q as expressed

37:04.940 --> 37:07.620
in the world reference frame into the camera reference

37:07.620 --> 37:12.300
frame, which basically means that the q, as before,

37:12.300 --> 37:19.900
can be written as a rotation matrix times Pw.

37:19.900 --> 37:22.300
And this expresses the full transformation

37:22.300 --> 37:24.060
between coordinates in the world reference

37:24.060 --> 37:28.500
frame to coordinates in the camera reference frame.

37:28.500 --> 37:30.580
Basically the same as before, but we

37:30.580 --> 37:34.100
had to account for the fact that the origins of the two

37:34.100 --> 37:37.060
reference frames are different, and that difference

37:37.060 --> 37:38.700
is captured by a translation vector.

37:44.500 --> 37:45.980
Translate first, right?

37:45.980 --> 37:47.820
Before, it would be the same?

37:47.820 --> 37:48.340
Yeah, sure.

37:52.100 --> 37:56.260
So all of this to say that given,

37:56.260 --> 37:58.140
assume that we know t and r.

37:58.140 --> 38:00.700
So assume that we know what is the relative position

38:00.700 --> 38:02.580
between the two origins.

38:02.580 --> 38:05.020
And we know how one reference frame

38:05.020 --> 38:07.220
is rotated with respect to the other reference frame.

38:07.220 --> 38:09.060
Then if you give me the coordinates in one reference

38:09.060 --> 38:11.020
frame, I can transform those coordinates

38:11.020 --> 38:14.220
in the other reference frame by using this rotation, which

38:14.220 --> 38:15.660
is a translation plus a rotation.

38:21.740 --> 38:30.180
In reality, oftentimes you use the equation in reverse,

38:30.180 --> 38:34.940
meaning that you try to estimate from a sequence of images

38:34.940 --> 38:37.580
what r and t are, which allows you

38:37.580 --> 38:40.380
to understand how your camera is moving with respect

38:40.380 --> 38:41.780
to the work frame.

38:41.780 --> 38:44.620
And that is a process that, for example,

38:44.620 --> 38:46.300
allow you to understand from images

38:46.300 --> 38:48.940
how the robot is moving through a procedure also referred

38:48.940 --> 38:51.420
to as visual autometry, what it will use on Mars rovers

38:51.420 --> 38:52.700
as well.

38:52.700 --> 38:55.260
We'll see that in more detail in a second.

38:55.260 --> 38:59.860
Now, the idea in order now to put everything together

38:59.860 --> 39:04.260
is to express the coordinate transformation also

39:04.260 --> 39:08.380
in the framework of homogeneous coordinates.

39:08.380 --> 39:09.700
And in particular, by considering

39:09.700 --> 39:15.260
homogeneous coordinates, let's represent

39:15.260 --> 39:19.900
a Pc in terms of its homogeneous representation.

39:19.900 --> 39:22.580
And then let's represent a Pw in terms

39:22.580 --> 39:25.460
of its homogeneous representation.

39:25.460 --> 39:29.380
Then the equation, the coordinate transformation

39:29.420 --> 39:37.700
can be clearly written as r, t, 0, 1, where 0, 3 times 1

39:37.700 --> 39:43.380
is a vector with three elements of zeros.

39:43.380 --> 39:45.420
So if you write down the multiplication,

39:45.420 --> 39:47.180
you will see immediately that what you get

39:47.180 --> 39:49.140
is exactly what we had.

39:49.140 --> 39:51.060
One more thing.

39:51.060 --> 39:53.300
Yeah, sure, of course.

39:53.300 --> 39:59.140
Is this a row vector?

39:59.140 --> 40:04.220
So this will be basically 0, 0, 0.

40:04.220 --> 40:07.500
So as pointed out, it should be 1 by 3.

40:07.500 --> 40:08.860
One row and three columns.

40:08.860 --> 40:10.020
Yeah, thanks.

40:14.900 --> 40:18.700
So the result will be Pc is equal to rPw plus t.

40:18.700 --> 40:21.300
The last component will be 1.

40:21.300 --> 40:23.060
So once you divide by the last component,

40:23.060 --> 40:26.380
you obtain exactly what we have.

40:26.380 --> 40:27.900
All right, so now we're in a position

40:27.940 --> 40:29.140
to put everything together.

40:29.140 --> 40:30.660
Finally, we found a transformation

40:30.660 --> 40:33.540
from points expressed with respect

40:33.540 --> 40:35.780
to world reference frame and points expressed

40:35.780 --> 40:37.620
in the pixel coordinates.

40:37.620 --> 40:39.860
And the full expression is expression

40:39.860 --> 40:43.260
that goes from the point as expressed

40:43.260 --> 40:45.820
in the camera reference system, then pixel coordinates

40:45.820 --> 40:47.300
through the camera matrix.

40:47.300 --> 40:48.700
But now we also have the relation

40:48.700 --> 40:51.180
between the point in the camera reference system

40:51.180 --> 40:53.340
and the point in the world reference system

40:53.340 --> 40:54.900
through the rotation and translation.

40:54.900 --> 41:00.940
We put everything together, and that's the end result.

41:00.940 --> 41:01.940
Any question about this?

41:01.940 --> 41:03.820
This is really by chaining all the transformation

41:03.820 --> 41:04.820
that we've found so far.

41:08.780 --> 41:13.860
So in particular, we can multiply this matrix

41:13.860 --> 41:14.700
by this matrix.

41:14.700 --> 41:20.220
So this will become r plus t, and this will be 0.

41:20.220 --> 41:22.340
And the end result is this equation.

41:22.340 --> 41:24.100
Basically, ph.

41:24.100 --> 41:29.020
Again, ph means the projected point in pixel coordinates

41:29.020 --> 41:33.260
as expressed in homogeneous pixel coordinates.

41:33.260 --> 41:51.740
Ph is equal to krt capital Ph.

41:51.780 --> 41:52.260
OK, work.

41:56.540 --> 41:58.380
Is this clear to everyone?

41:58.380 --> 42:00.860
Let me know, because otherwise the calibration process becomes

42:00.860 --> 42:02.260
impossible to understand.

42:05.260 --> 42:08.700
Is lowercase p the camera frame?

42:08.700 --> 42:10.460
Is what?

42:10.460 --> 42:12.620
Lowercase p is the camera frame?

42:12.620 --> 42:15.820
These are the coordinates in pixels.

42:15.820 --> 42:17.420
So we would ask it to.

42:17.420 --> 42:17.980
What's that?

42:17.980 --> 42:18.820
Full transformation.

42:18.820 --> 42:20.700
Full transformation, yeah.

42:20.700 --> 42:24.900
And basically, the translation with respect to the corner

42:24.900 --> 42:29.260
and the pixelization is taken care of by the camera matrix,

42:29.260 --> 42:31.260
as we discussed before.

42:31.260 --> 42:33.020
So this is really the full transformation.

42:33.020 --> 42:38.020
You give me coordinates like 3 meters for x, 4 meters for y,

42:38.020 --> 42:41.700
with respect to a board reference frame.

42:41.700 --> 42:43.700
And here you get pixel 3 and pixel 4.

42:43.700 --> 42:49.860
All right.

42:49.860 --> 42:53.780
So this matrix, referred to as the camera matrix,

42:53.780 --> 42:56.740
contains the so-called intrinsic parameters of the cameras.

42:56.740 --> 42:58.660
So there's interesting properties of the cameras

42:58.660 --> 43:01.180
in terms of focal length, displacement

43:01.180 --> 43:05.300
of the original reference system in the image plane

43:05.300 --> 43:07.740
with respect to the pixel coordinate reference frame,

43:07.740 --> 43:09.020
and so on.

43:09.020 --> 43:11.060
R is a rotation.

43:11.060 --> 43:14.500
It expresses the rotation between the word reference

43:14.500 --> 43:16.740
frame and the camera reference frame.

43:16.740 --> 43:18.900
And t is a translation.

43:18.900 --> 43:21.340
Basically, if you think that when

43:21.340 --> 43:23.700
you start the operation of your robot,

43:23.700 --> 43:26.220
the original word reference frame coincides with the camera

43:26.220 --> 43:28.420
reference frame, this is basically the translation

43:28.420 --> 43:30.220
that your robot has been undergoing.

43:33.300 --> 43:39.180
So in principle, if you knew this transformation,

43:39.180 --> 43:41.420
and as we will discuss later, you

43:41.420 --> 43:47.420
knew projected points in maybe different images,

43:47.420 --> 43:50.220
for example, through stereo vision or other procedure,

43:50.220 --> 43:53.300
then you can reconstruct where that point was

43:53.300 --> 43:55.740
in the word frame.

43:55.740 --> 43:57.700
That's how, for example, this equation

43:57.700 --> 44:01.020
is used in order to reconstruct the 3D environment by only

44:01.020 --> 44:03.020
looking at the 2D images.

44:03.020 --> 44:07.020
By the way, this is also what your brain does.

44:07.020 --> 44:08.780
The problem, though, is in general,

44:08.780 --> 44:11.580
you don't know k, you don't know r, you don't know t.

44:15.140 --> 44:19.460
So this leads to the so-called calibration procedure.

44:19.460 --> 44:21.540
The goal of the calibration procedure

44:21.540 --> 44:25.540
is to determine that projection transformation.

44:25.540 --> 44:28.740
And in particular, to pinpoint the intrinsic values

44:28.740 --> 44:30.100
of your camera, then later on you

44:30.100 --> 44:33.460
can use for scene reconstruction.

44:33.460 --> 44:35.940
So the idea of camera calibration

44:35.940 --> 44:38.540
is relatively straightforward, making

44:38.540 --> 44:42.700
it work is extremely, well, not extremely,

44:42.700 --> 44:45.300
but it's challenging and requires some experience.

44:45.300 --> 44:48.540
And the first problem in the PSET3

44:48.540 --> 44:54.700
will give you an opportunity to test the calibration

44:54.700 --> 45:00.100
procedure on some sample images to see how all of this works.

45:00.100 --> 45:02.980
So the idea of the calibration process is as follows.

45:02.980 --> 45:06.100
We are given a pattern.

45:06.140 --> 45:10.580
So for example, if you go in robotics labs,

45:10.580 --> 45:14.540
oftentimes you might have seen a checkboard type of patterns.

45:14.540 --> 45:16.260
Those are used to calibrate cameras.

45:18.900 --> 45:21.620
And you will be working with that for your homework number

45:21.620 --> 45:22.980
three.

45:22.980 --> 45:27.140
All right, so in a way, it's like vaccination.

45:27.140 --> 45:29.700
The first time you do it is a little bit unpleasant,

45:29.700 --> 45:31.020
but once you do it once or twice,

45:31.020 --> 45:33.300
then you're pretty much set for life.

45:33.300 --> 45:35.580
But the first time, it's unpleasant.

45:35.620 --> 45:38.140
So you're given this checkboard or pattern.

45:38.140 --> 45:39.580
So here is a three-dimensional one.

45:39.580 --> 45:41.660
It could be a planar one and so on.

45:41.660 --> 45:44.660
You have a word reference system that

45:44.660 --> 45:48.300
is placed in a known position with respect to this pattern.

45:48.300 --> 45:51.420
For example, here, the origin will be this corner,

