59:38.120 --> 59:41.640
to have a 2n greater than 11, the parameters

59:41.640 --> 59:43.840
that you want to determine.

59:43.840 --> 59:46.360
In general, you want to have more so that your calibration

59:46.400 --> 59:51.560
procedure is robust.

59:51.560 --> 59:54.480
This means that at least you want to have six correspondences

59:54.480 --> 59:57.200
in reality more.

59:57.200 --> 01:00:01.560
OK, so then how do we solve the problem?

01:00:01.560 --> 01:00:05.440
As I said, this matrix m is defined up

01:00:05.440 --> 01:00:07.280
to a scale factor due to the fact

01:00:07.280 --> 01:00:11.240
that we work with homogeneous coordinates.

01:00:11.240 --> 01:00:15.440
In particular, as you can see from here,

01:00:15.440 --> 01:00:20.760
if m is a solution to that system of linear equations,

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:25.560
say m bar, then lambda m bar is also a solution.

01:00:25.560 --> 01:00:28.040
So we can just fix the scale in order

01:00:28.040 --> 01:00:30.480
to deal with this ambiguity.

01:00:30.480 --> 01:00:33.960
And of course, m bar equals 0 is always a solution,

01:00:33.960 --> 01:00:36.080
but it will be the trivial solution, not something

01:00:36.080 --> 01:00:37.800
we're interested in, because it will not

01:00:37.800 --> 01:00:39.400
represent a physical column.

01:00:39.400 --> 01:00:42.120
So we're interested in a non-zero solution

01:00:42.120 --> 01:00:43.760
for which we fix the scale.

01:00:43.760 --> 01:00:48.800
For example, we can fix the scale at m over bar equal 1.

01:00:48.800 --> 01:00:52.400
So basically, we take the m vector such

01:00:52.400 --> 01:00:55.480
that the norm is equal to 1.

01:00:55.480 --> 01:00:58.600
All right, we said that in general, we

01:00:58.600 --> 01:01:05.440
want to have a number of equations that is

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:07.640
much larger than 11.

01:01:07.640 --> 01:01:09.480
And in general, due to measurement error,

01:01:09.480 --> 01:01:12.000
p tilde m is not exactly equal to 0.

01:01:12.040 --> 01:01:14.080
So the problem that in the end we

01:01:14.080 --> 01:01:16.480
want to solve with the calibration procedure

01:01:16.480 --> 01:01:23.000
is to find the minimum 12-dimensional vector m such

01:01:23.000 --> 01:01:28.560
that the norm of a p tilde m is minimized.

01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:31.960
We want to make it 0 oftentimes due to measurement error,

01:01:31.960 --> 01:01:35.560
but at least we want to make it as small as possible,

01:01:35.560 --> 01:01:45.520
such that the norm of m is equal to 1.

01:01:45.520 --> 01:01:46.880
That's the problem we want to solve.

01:01:50.960 --> 01:01:51.460
Yeah?

01:01:51.460 --> 01:01:53.160
Then what do we mean by the assumption

01:01:53.160 --> 01:01:55.000
that m is equal to 1?

01:01:55.000 --> 01:02:00.120
So if m is a solution, lambda m is also a solution.

01:02:00.120 --> 01:02:03.760
So let's just pick the scale once and for all,

01:02:03.800 --> 01:02:06.200
and then we solve with respect to that scale.

01:02:06.200 --> 01:02:08.720
If you want, you can make m equal to 4.

01:02:08.720 --> 01:02:10.080
It's just to fix the scale.

01:02:10.080 --> 01:02:12.000
Or m equal to 5, any number.

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:14.880
We can make m equal to lambda.

01:02:14.880 --> 01:02:21.920
But 1 just leads to cleaner mathematics.

01:02:21.920 --> 01:02:25.120
So how do we solve this problem?

01:02:25.120 --> 01:02:28.240
Well, so for the graduate students among you,

01:02:28.240 --> 01:02:30.720
if you're undergrad, this is like a bonus.

01:02:30.720 --> 01:02:32.920
If you're a graduate student, this is not a bonus.

01:02:32.920 --> 01:02:34.160
You should know it.

01:02:34.160 --> 01:02:37.280
So this is a constrained optimization problem.

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:41.720
So as we saw for the lecture on indirect methods,

01:02:41.720 --> 01:02:43.040
the way these problems are solved

01:02:43.040 --> 01:02:45.040
is by forming a Lagrangian.

01:02:45.040 --> 01:02:49.880
So the Lagrangian L is equal to m transpose p t

01:02:49.880 --> 01:02:52.280
to the transpose of p m.

01:02:52.280 --> 01:02:56.160
I've just expanded the norm.

01:02:56.160 --> 01:03:02.760
Plus Lagrange multiplier lambda times 1 minus m transpose m.

01:03:02.920 --> 01:03:07.640
So the minimum is a critical point of the Lagrangian.

01:03:07.640 --> 01:03:11.680
So the derivative of the Lagrangian with respect to m

01:03:11.680 --> 01:03:19.640
is equal to 2 p m plus, actually minus, minus 2m.

01:03:19.640 --> 01:03:21.000
And this has to be equal to 0.

01:03:21.000 --> 01:03:23.400
It has to be a critical point of the Lagrangian.

01:03:23.400 --> 01:03:27.400
Which means lambda m.

01:03:27.400 --> 01:03:31.760
Which means that a necessary condition for a vector m

01:03:31.800 --> 01:03:36.880
to be a minimizer is that the p m is equal to lambda m.

01:03:36.880 --> 01:03:39.400
Can you see?

01:03:39.400 --> 01:03:42.320
Which means that m has to be an eigenvector of,

01:03:42.320 --> 01:03:47.080
so we call this p tilde, m has to be an eigenvector of p tilde.

01:03:47.080 --> 01:03:50.120
So what we do, we compute the eigenvalues of p tilde

01:03:50.120 --> 01:03:52.040
and then the corresponding eigenvectors.

01:03:52.040 --> 01:03:53.880
Among those, there is a minimizer.

01:03:53.880 --> 01:03:56.360
But which one is the minimizer?

01:03:56.360 --> 01:04:05.760
Well, let's take, let lambda be an eigenvector eigenvalue pair.

01:04:05.760 --> 01:04:09.280
Then I plug that into the cost function.

01:04:11.880 --> 01:04:18.160
The cost function is m transpose p tilde p m.

01:04:18.160 --> 01:04:20.160
But since you know that m and lambda

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:22.200
is an eigenvector eigenvalue pair, which

01:04:22.200 --> 01:04:25.600
means that p m is equal to lambda m,

01:04:25.600 --> 01:04:34.120
this is equal to m transpose lambda square m.

01:04:34.120 --> 01:04:37.440
But we have the constraint that the norm of m is equal to 1.

01:04:37.440 --> 01:04:40.240
So this is actually equal to lambda square.

01:04:40.240 --> 01:04:45.000
So among all the eigenvectors, which one do we pick?

01:04:45.000 --> 01:04:45.500
One?

01:04:45.500 --> 01:04:46.000
Yeah.

01:04:46.000 --> 01:04:49.040
We pick the one that corresponds to the smallest eigenvalues.

01:04:49.040 --> 01:04:51.120
So basically, a calibration procedure

01:04:51.120 --> 01:04:53.720
entails forming this matrix p tilde,

01:04:53.720 --> 01:04:55.540
computing the eigenvalues and eigenvectors.

01:04:55.540 --> 01:04:57.840
For example, with a single value decomposition,

01:04:57.840 --> 01:05:01.640
picking the eigenvectors with a minimum eigenvalue.

01:05:01.640 --> 01:05:04.120
All right, so we found how to compute m.

01:05:04.120 --> 01:05:05.120
Yes?

01:05:05.120 --> 01:05:07.600
And that's also the singular value decomposition,

01:05:07.600 --> 01:05:10.080
ensuring that m is orthogonal, right?

01:05:10.080 --> 01:05:10.580
Yeah.

01:05:10.580 --> 01:05:12.560
But, I mean, what can you do?

01:05:12.560 --> 01:05:16.040
Actually recover the matrix directly.

01:05:16.040 --> 01:05:18.040
You really like to go one step.

01:05:18.040 --> 01:05:19.040
Because it's there.

01:05:19.040 --> 01:05:20.480
That's what we're doing here.

01:05:20.480 --> 01:05:21.200
Yes.

01:05:21.200 --> 01:05:21.700
Yeah.

01:05:21.700 --> 01:05:23.320
But you're thinking the right direction.

01:05:23.320 --> 01:05:33.280
So now we have m, which is a 12, sorry, is a 3 by 4 matrix.

01:05:33.280 --> 01:05:38.960
But we want now to compute the individual components k r

01:05:38.960 --> 01:05:40.640
and k t.

01:05:43.160 --> 01:05:44.560
How do we do that?

01:05:44.560 --> 01:05:50.800
Well, we can partition m, because at the end of the day,

01:05:50.800 --> 01:05:53.080
that's what we want to compute, k r and t.

01:05:53.880 --> 01:06:10.160
So we can partition m as a 3 by 3 matrix and a 3 by 1 vector.

01:06:12.840 --> 01:06:14.840
So we know this, and we know this.

01:06:14.840 --> 01:06:18.920
From this a, we want to compute k r.

01:06:18.920 --> 01:06:20.440
So we want to be able to separate

01:06:20.440 --> 01:06:22.720
the intrinsic from the rotation.

01:06:22.760 --> 01:06:23.840
How do we do that?

01:06:23.840 --> 01:06:26.560
Well, we use a fact in linear algebra

01:06:26.560 --> 01:06:30.520
that is referred to as a q r and r q decomposition, which

01:06:30.520 --> 01:06:32.560
is a little bit unfortunate name in this context.

01:06:32.560 --> 01:06:38.900
But basically, the idea is that every n by n matrix

01:06:38.900 --> 01:06:45.800
can be decomposed into an orthogonal matrix q

01:06:45.800 --> 01:06:48.880
and an upper triangular matrix r.

01:06:48.880 --> 01:06:54.720
So this can be decomposed as r times q,

01:06:54.720 --> 01:06:57.040
where r is upper triangular.

01:06:59.960 --> 01:07:03.600
This is a fact from linear algebra, and this is orthogonal.

01:07:06.920 --> 01:07:09.640
So basically, there is a line.

01:07:09.640 --> 01:07:11.080
I mean, there is a function in NumPy

01:07:11.080 --> 01:07:14.200
that you can call in order to do this rq decomposition.

01:07:14.200 --> 01:07:15.720
The reason why this is unfortunate

01:07:15.720 --> 01:07:18.040
is that r is not the rotation matrix.

01:07:18.080 --> 01:07:20.120
This is the upper triangular matrix.

01:07:20.120 --> 01:07:25.400
So the r in our context will be this q, and this will be k.

01:07:25.400 --> 01:07:29.520
Unfortunately, it's called rq decomposition,

01:07:29.520 --> 01:07:32.760
but just to make sure that you are not confused by the terms.

01:07:35.640 --> 01:07:38.800
So once you have computed the rotation matrix, which

01:07:38.800 --> 01:07:41.840
will be the q there, and the upper triangular matrix, which

01:07:41.840 --> 01:07:45.960
corresponds to the camera matrix, which is the r there,

01:07:45.960 --> 01:07:51.880
how do you compute a, which will be the translation vector?

01:07:51.880 --> 01:07:57.800
Well, you know from here that a is equal to kt.

01:07:57.800 --> 01:07:59.800
So to compute t, what do you do?

01:07:59.800 --> 01:08:01.600
Now that you know k?

01:08:01.600 --> 01:08:02.400
You're the inverse.

01:08:02.400 --> 01:08:06.120
Yeah, it's a technique.

01:08:06.120 --> 01:08:09.920
So that's how you go from pairs of correspondences

01:08:09.920 --> 01:08:14.280
to an intrinsic and extrinsic camera.

01:08:15.200 --> 01:08:18.080
You'll be exploring this further in your p-set.

01:08:22.080 --> 01:08:23.960
Fortunately, it looks relatively simple,

01:08:23.960 --> 01:08:25.760
but there are some nuances that you'll see.

01:08:33.600 --> 01:08:35.280
So there is an additional complication

01:08:35.280 --> 01:08:38.280
that we won't see in this class, but just for you to know,

01:08:38.280 --> 01:08:42.360
is that here we have assumed basically a linear model,

01:08:42.360 --> 01:08:46.920
meaning that we don't have a magnification due to the fact

01:08:46.920 --> 01:08:50.840
that we're using a lens and not a pinhole camera in our image.

01:08:50.840 --> 01:08:54.040
But in reality, you might have magnification

01:08:54.040 --> 01:08:56.760
as you move away from the center of your image.

01:08:56.760 --> 01:09:00.280
If we have a magnification that is larger at the center

01:09:00.280 --> 01:09:03.680
and goes farther as you go away, like for a fisheye camera,

01:09:03.680 --> 01:09:05.920
then we talk about barrel distortion.

01:09:05.920 --> 01:09:09.320
Or conversely, we talk about pin cushion distortion.

01:09:09.320 --> 01:09:12.520
So in general, cameras also have a distortion factor

01:09:12.520 --> 01:09:14.520
that you have to account for, which

01:09:14.520 --> 01:09:16.960
makes the calibration process even more complicated,

01:09:16.960 --> 01:09:19.840
because now you also have to infer the distortion factor

01:09:19.840 --> 01:09:22.880
from the calibration procedure.

01:09:22.880 --> 01:09:25.720
Usually, fortunately, apart from fisheye cameras

01:09:25.720 --> 01:09:27.760
and specific cameras, this distortion factor

01:09:27.760 --> 01:09:28.440
is quite small.

01:09:31.120 --> 01:09:33.280
So this estimation is beyond the scope of this class,

01:09:33.280 --> 01:09:35.280
but just for you to know that in full generality,

01:09:35.280 --> 01:09:37.840
we should also account for an additional parameter.

01:09:37.840 --> 01:09:38.340
Yeah?

01:09:38.340 --> 01:09:39.900
If you're working with cameras and you

01:09:39.900 --> 01:09:43.540
buy a particular model of camera or image sensor,

01:09:43.540 --> 01:09:46.540
do you have to recalibrate every single particular sensor

01:09:46.540 --> 01:09:48.780
or just when you have a new model of sensor?

01:09:48.780 --> 01:09:51.060
Oh, yeah, they calibrate every single camera.

01:09:51.060 --> 01:09:52.020
Oh, yeah, pretty much.

01:09:52.020 --> 01:09:57.780
Yeah, use small manufacturing differences in general.

01:10:01.780 --> 01:10:05.180
All right, so now we have done all of that.

01:10:05.220 --> 01:10:12.820
So we have a good understanding of how points in the world

01:10:12.820 --> 01:10:15.780
are projected onto a camera.

01:10:15.780 --> 01:10:17.220
How do we use that information?

01:10:17.220 --> 01:10:19.780
Well, the very first way to use that information

01:10:19.780 --> 01:10:23.260
is trying to recover structure, meaning now you only

01:10:23.260 --> 01:10:24.180
have images.

01:10:24.180 --> 01:10:28.380
You want to build a 3D representation of the world.

01:10:28.380 --> 01:10:30.740
So the question is, if a given image,

01:10:30.740 --> 01:10:36.700
and now you have estimated your perspective projection

01:10:36.700 --> 01:10:41.140
matrix and so on, can you recover the point P, capital

01:10:41.140 --> 01:10:45.420
P, that gave rise to a given point P in your image plane?

01:10:45.420 --> 01:10:48.940
If you do that, now you're able to reconstruct the environment.

01:10:48.940 --> 01:10:51.540
The answer is unfortunately no, which

01:10:51.540 --> 01:10:55.340
is one of the big challenges in computer vision.

01:10:55.340 --> 01:10:56.900
And the problem is due to the fact

01:10:56.940 --> 01:11:01.620
that you have an inherent death ambiguity in terms of where

01:11:01.620 --> 01:11:03.460
the point P could be.

01:11:03.460 --> 01:11:06.380
Through our perspective equation,

01:11:06.380 --> 01:11:11.660
what we basically found is that any point along the ray that

01:11:11.660 --> 01:11:15.460
connects the projected point to the pinhole

01:11:15.460 --> 01:11:18.020
could be a possible point in the 3D world

01:11:18.020 --> 01:11:22.020
that gave rise to this point P. Meaning that a point here,

01:11:22.020 --> 01:11:24.900
or a point here, or a point here, or a point here,

01:11:24.900 --> 01:11:27.620
will give rise exactly to the same projected point

01:11:27.620 --> 01:11:29.140
in the image plane.

01:11:29.140 --> 01:11:33.380
Which means that the only thing that you can infer

01:11:33.380 --> 01:11:36.220
is that the point in 3D that might have generated

01:11:36.220 --> 01:11:38.500
this point, small p, is somewhere along this line,

01:11:38.500 --> 01:11:41.820
but you don't know exactly where.

01:11:41.820 --> 01:11:43.340
In other words, if you go to Italy

01:11:43.340 --> 01:11:44.820
and you take this famous picture,

01:11:44.820 --> 01:11:47.260
it seems that everyone goes to Pisa to take this image

01:11:47.260 --> 01:11:50.060
for whatever reason.

01:11:50.060 --> 01:11:54.700
In principle, you can really say if the Pisa Tower

01:11:54.700 --> 01:11:56.940
is bigger or smaller than this person.

01:11:56.940 --> 01:11:59.140
As a human, you can, but because you're

01:11:59.140 --> 01:12:01.460
using a high-level understanding of the camera.

01:12:01.460 --> 01:12:03.100
So basically, you are relating.

01:12:03.100 --> 01:12:05.460
You're saying, well, this will also be people.

01:12:05.460 --> 01:12:08.260
So since this is small, it means that this is in the background

01:12:08.260 --> 01:12:09.820
and this is in the foreground.

01:12:09.820 --> 01:12:12.820
But without that level of semantic understanding,

01:12:12.820 --> 01:12:14.820
just by looking at this image, you cannot really

01:12:14.820 --> 01:12:17.820
cover the scale of the scene.

01:12:17.820 --> 01:12:19.660
Which means that you do something more.

01:12:19.660 --> 01:12:22.220
And basically, there are four main ways

01:12:22.220 --> 01:12:26.060
of recovering structure, meaning death in the scene,

01:12:26.060 --> 01:12:29.500
from planner, from 2D images.

01:12:29.500 --> 01:12:33.540
The first one is through the recognition of landmarks

01:12:33.540 --> 01:12:36.580
or semantic understanding, what I was explaining before.

01:12:36.580 --> 01:12:38.820
And it's something that goes beyond the scope

01:12:38.820 --> 01:12:40.100
of this class.

01:12:40.100 --> 01:12:42.260
The second one is that from focus.

01:12:42.260 --> 01:12:45.700
As we will see in a second, the idea is to take images

01:12:45.700 --> 01:12:48.380
until when the image is perfectly in focus

01:12:48.380 --> 01:12:50.820
and by using a thin lens equation,

01:12:50.820 --> 01:12:56.540
extrapolate what the depth of the image there is.

01:12:56.540 --> 01:12:58.060
We'll see that this is in a second.

01:12:58.060 --> 01:13:00.180
Stereo vision, stereo vision that

01:13:00.180 --> 01:13:03.860
relies on using two cameras with a known displacement

01:13:03.860 --> 01:13:06.740
and rotation between the two and known camera matrices

01:13:06.740 --> 01:13:10.620
in order to reconstruct scenes through a procedure referred

01:13:10.620 --> 01:13:12.100
to as triangulation.

01:13:12.100 --> 01:13:15.020
In particular, the idea is to take two distant images

01:13:15.020 --> 01:13:18.580
at the same time and fuse the information

01:13:18.580 --> 01:13:19.940
in order to reconstruct that.

01:13:19.940 --> 01:13:22.180
That's what you do as a human.

01:13:22.180 --> 01:13:23.540
Or a structure from motion.

01:13:23.540 --> 01:13:27.700
So now you take multiple images at different times,

01:13:27.700 --> 01:13:32.500
and then you try to reconstruct the scene by using that.

01:13:32.500 --> 01:13:35.460
So let's start with the depth from focus.

01:13:35.460 --> 01:13:38.940
The depth from focus idea is fairly simple

01:13:38.940 --> 01:13:39.860
but very cumbersome.

01:13:39.860 --> 01:13:42.580
So most of the times, this is not used.

01:13:42.580 --> 01:13:48.300
The idea is that you take an image,

01:13:48.300 --> 01:13:51.580
but you change the focus in such a way

01:13:51.580 --> 01:13:55.940
that the image becomes as sharp as possible, which

01:13:55.940 --> 01:13:58.260
means that the image is in focus.

01:13:58.260 --> 01:14:01.100
Which means that basically you have it pinpointed at the z

01:14:01.100 --> 01:14:03.940
value for your thin lens.

01:14:03.940 --> 01:14:06.980
Now from last time, you know the thin lens equation.

01:14:06.980 --> 01:14:09.220
So if you know the focal length, and if you

01:14:09.220 --> 01:14:13.340
know the depth in terms of the image plane

01:14:13.340 --> 01:14:15.780
at which the image is in focus, then

01:14:15.780 --> 01:14:17.340
by using the thin lens equation, you

01:14:17.380 --> 01:14:19.460
can then infer capital Z, which is

01:14:19.460 --> 01:14:24.020
the distance between the camera and the point in the 3D world.

01:14:24.020 --> 01:14:25.500
But that means that for each point,

01:14:25.500 --> 01:14:27.980
you have to do this business of putting a point in focus,

01:14:27.980 --> 01:14:30.540
so it's a bit cumbersome.

01:14:30.540 --> 01:14:33.140
So the standard way, or one of the two standard ways,

01:14:33.140 --> 01:14:35.340
the other standard one will be structure from motion.

01:14:35.340 --> 01:14:39.340
In order to recover structure, it is to use a stereo vision.

01:14:39.340 --> 01:14:40.820
Stereo vision, as I said, entails,

01:14:40.820 --> 01:14:44.060
and that's what your brain does, to take

01:14:44.060 --> 01:14:47.320
an image with two cameras, two eyes in your case,

01:14:47.320 --> 01:14:50.600
and exploit the disparity or the differences

01:14:50.600 --> 01:14:53.200
between analogous points in the two images

01:14:53.200 --> 01:14:55.080
in order to reconstruct the scene

01:14:55.080 --> 01:14:59.000
by using a process known as a triangulation.

01:14:59.000 --> 01:15:01.760
A triangulation process is fairly intuitive.

01:15:01.760 --> 01:15:03.040
So this is the image plane.

01:15:03.040 --> 01:15:05.720
This is the pinhole for the first camera, image plane

01:15:05.720 --> 01:15:08.040
and pinhole for the second camera.

01:15:08.040 --> 01:15:12.240
It is a point P that has been projected in the first camera

01:15:12.240 --> 01:15:13.560
and in the second camera.

01:15:13.560 --> 01:15:16.640
P and P prime are the projections.

01:15:16.680 --> 01:15:19.040
Now if you only have one image, the only thing

01:15:19.040 --> 01:15:22.240
that you can say is that the point capital P

01:15:22.240 --> 01:15:24.760
lies somewhere along this ray.

01:15:25.920 --> 01:15:27.800
And then if you only have this image,

01:15:27.800 --> 01:15:30.880
the only thing that you can say is that the point capital P

01:15:30.880 --> 01:15:33.520
lies somewhere along this ray.

01:15:34.440 --> 01:15:36.380
But now if you have two images,

01:15:37.320 --> 01:15:39.400
then since you know that the point capital P

01:15:39.400 --> 01:15:41.020
has to lie along this ray,

01:15:41.020 --> 01:15:44.120
and the point capital P has to lie along this ray,

01:15:44.120 --> 01:15:46.240
then the intersection is really the position

01:15:46.240 --> 01:15:49.100
of the point in a 3D world.

01:15:49.100 --> 01:15:51.400
And that's how, by fusing information,

01:15:51.400 --> 01:15:53.840
by using this procedure referred to as triangulation,

01:15:53.840 --> 01:15:57.460
you can pinpoint where the point is in the 3D world.

01:15:57.460 --> 01:15:59.040
Of course, in order to do this, you

01:15:59.040 --> 01:16:03.520
need to know what is the position of this image

01:16:03.520 --> 01:16:05.200
plane with respect to this image plane.

01:16:05.200 --> 01:16:07.420
So basically, to know the position of this camera

01:16:07.420 --> 01:16:09.880
reference system with respect to this camera reference system,

01:16:09.880 --> 01:16:11.500
and also know the intrinsic parameters

01:16:11.500 --> 01:16:15.640
to both the first camera and the second camera.

01:16:15.640 --> 01:16:18.060
So there is also a procedure to calibrate the stereo rig,

01:16:18.060 --> 01:16:20.860
as opposed to a monocular camera.

01:16:20.860 --> 01:16:23.620
Now, the last thing is that, in reality,

01:16:23.620 --> 01:16:30.020
due to measurement noise and other inaccuracies,

01:16:30.020 --> 01:16:35.940
a point, capital P, may map into two points, P and P prime,

01:16:35.940 --> 01:16:38.620
such that when you extend the rays outward,

01:16:38.620 --> 01:16:41.060
they do not intersect at the point, capital P,

01:16:41.100 --> 01:16:44.360
because of measurement error.

01:16:44.360 --> 01:16:45.500
In principle, they should.

01:16:45.500 --> 01:16:48.940
So if our model was perfect, they

01:16:48.940 --> 01:16:50.660
should intersect at capital P. And that's

01:16:50.660 --> 01:16:52.240
what we would do for triangulation.

01:16:52.240 --> 01:16:54.500
But in general, they may not.

01:16:54.500 --> 01:16:59.060
So in reality, in order to triangulate, what you do is to,

01:16:59.060 --> 01:17:00.740
there are a few different ways to do it.

01:17:00.740 --> 01:17:06.200
But a common one is to consider the estimation of P

01:17:06.200 --> 01:17:08.580
by considering the so-called reprojection error.

01:17:08.580 --> 01:17:12.300
So basically, we try to find the point in the 3D board,

01:17:12.300 --> 01:17:18.580
so the point Q, such that its projection in the first image

01:17:18.580 --> 01:17:21.500
and the second image gives rise to the lowest possible

01:17:21.500 --> 01:17:25.220
reprojection error, meaning the distance between the projection

01:17:25.220 --> 01:17:27.460
that we have observed and the projection

01:17:27.460 --> 01:17:32.300
that our estimate of P that we call Q will give.

01:17:32.300 --> 01:17:35.220
Ideally, we would like for this distance to be 0,

01:17:35.220 --> 01:17:38.140
which will be the case if the two original rays were

01:17:38.180 --> 01:17:38.860
to intersect.

01:17:38.860 --> 01:17:39.740
In reality, we can't.

01:17:39.740 --> 01:17:43.120
We just have to find the best estimate.

01:17:43.120 --> 01:17:45.800
Does it make sense?

01:17:45.800 --> 01:17:46.540
Cool.

01:17:46.540 --> 01:17:48.060
One of the challenges to your vision

01:17:48.060 --> 01:17:52.420
is, how do you know that the point P here corresponds

01:17:52.420 --> 01:17:54.380
to this point P prime here?

01:17:54.380 --> 01:17:55.960
That's why it's very important to find

01:17:55.960 --> 01:17:57.700
a feature that is a bit distinctive,

01:17:57.700 --> 01:18:00.180
so you can make these correspondences.

01:18:00.180 --> 01:18:02.500
There are a number of structural properties

01:18:02.500 --> 01:18:04.540
of the stereo matching process that one can

01:18:04.540 --> 01:18:06.660
leverage to do that effectively.

01:18:06.700 --> 01:18:10.460
But that's something that we will discuss on Friday

01:18:10.460 --> 01:18:12.780
for those of you that are enrolled

01:18:12.780 --> 01:18:14.340
in the graduate version of this class.

01:18:14.340 --> 01:18:17.140
We will be talking about stereo vision and then

01:18:17.140 --> 01:18:19.300
the other procedure to reconstruct structure, which

01:18:19.300 --> 01:18:23.000
is structure from motion.

01:18:23.000 --> 01:18:24.620
So yeah, to me, it's late.

01:18:24.620 --> 01:18:28.820
Sorry, but see you tomorrow.

